      passage: "<p>Talia had promised her father she would be careful, and for the first two hours on the lake, careful was easy. The July morning was glassy and bright, the kind of stillness that made the water look like poured silver. She paddled her kayak past the reeds along the shoreline, checking the crawfish traps she and her cousin Devon had set the night before, while Devon stayed back at the campsite mending a torn tent flap. The forecast had promised sun until evening, so when the first breeze rattled the cattails, Talia barely noticed it.</p><p>By the time she noticed the sky, it was too late to pretend otherwise. A wall of gray-green cloud had swallowed the ridge behind the campsite, and the breeze had turned into a wind that shoved her kayak sideways. Thunder cracked somewhere close, not the distant rumble of a storm passing far off but the sharp, immediate kind that meant the storm was already arriving. Rain hit the lake in a hiss, and within moments the glassy water had become a field of whitecaps that slapped against the hull. Talia's arms went stiff. For a few seconds she simply gripped her paddle and did nothing at all, which she would later recognize as the most dangerous thing she could have done.</p><p>Her father's voice surfaced in her memory, calm and unhurried the way it always was when he talked about the water: point your bow into the waves, not away from them, and find the closest shore, not the familiar one. Talia forced a slow breath through her nose and turned the kayak's nose to meet the next swell head-on instead of letting it catch her broadside. The campsite was a mile back the way she had come, but a rocky point jutted out barely two hundred yards to her left. She aimed for it, paddling in short, controlled strokes, refusing to let her mind race ahead to what might happen if she capsized.</p><p>The wind fought her the whole way, and twice a wave broke over the bow and filled her lap with cold water, but she kept the kayak's nose steady and her strokes even. When the hull finally scraped against submerged rock, she nearly cried out with relief. She dragged the kayak up onto the point and crawled beneath a shelf of stone that leaned out over the shore like a low roof, and there she sat, soaked and shaking, while the storm emptied itself over the lake.</p><p>It passed in twenty minutes, the way summer storms often do, leaving the air rinsed and cool. Devon found her an hour later, working his way along the shoreline in the camp canoe, shouting her name until she answered. Talia noticed, as she climbed into his canoe, that her hands had finally stopped trembling. She had not done anything remarkable, she told herself, only remembered what she had been taught and refused to panic long enough to use it. But she also understood, paddling back toward camp beside her cousin, that she would never again think of the lake as simply calm or simply dangerous. It was both, always, and now so was she a little more prepared for either.</p>",
