      name: "Reading: Informational Text 1",
      passageTitle: "Robots at Work, Robots at Home",
      passage: "<p>When most people picture a robot, they imagine a humanoid machine walking and talking like a character from a movie. In reality, the robots that shape daily life rarely look human at all, and many of them are so embedded in ordinary routines that people barely notice them working. A dishwasher-sized arm welding car frames on an assembly line, a surgical device guided by a doctor's hands from across an operating room, a rover trundling across the dust of Mars: all of these count as robots, even though none of them resembble the machines of science fiction.</p><p>Manufacturing was one of the first industries to be transformed by robotics, and it remains one of the largest. Industrial robotic arms can repeat the same precise motion thousands of times a day without tiring, welding, painting, and assembling products with a consistency that human workers, however skilled, cannot match over long shifts. Car factories were early adopters of this technology, and today robotic arms build everything from smartphones to furniture. Supporters of automation argue that these machines free human workers from repetitive, physically strenuous tasks and reduce workplace injuries, while critics point out that the same technology has eliminated many factory jobs. Both claims are supported by evidence, and a careful reader has to weigh them rather than accepting either one automatically.</p><p>Medicine offers a different kind of robotic partnership, one in which the robot never acts alone. Surgical robots do not operate independently; instead, a trained surgeon controls the instruments remotely, and the robot translates the surgeon's hand movements into smaller, steadier motions than a human hand alone could achieve. This allows for incisions a fraction of the size of traditional surgical cuts, which often means less pain and a faster recovery for the patient. Robots also assist with rehabilitation, helping patients relearn to walk after an injury by providing support that gradually decreases as the patient's strength returns.</p><p>Exploration may be the field where robots do work that would otherwise be impossible for humans to do at all. Rovers have crossed the surface of Mars for years, sending back data from an environment where no person could survive without extraordinary protection, and underwater robots dive to ocean depths where the water pressure would crush a human-occupied submarine. These machines extend human curiosity into places our bodies cannot yet follow.</p><p>Even outside of laboratories and factories, robots have quietly entered ordinary homes. Robotic vacuums map a room and navigate around furniture; smart assistants respond to spoken commands; and delivery robots have begun rolling along sidewalks in some cities. None of these machines think or decide the way a human does, no matter how convincingly they seem to respond to their surroundings. They follow instructions written by programmers, however complicated those instructions may be. Understanding that distinction, between a machine that follows rules and a mind that reasons, is essential to understanding what robots can and cannot yet do.</p>",
